Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Offsets: an intersection of two offsets that runs all round a periodic
curve (a circle where a plane cuts a cylinder) is trimmed to the arcs over
the edges it is the image of, also where they cross the curve's origin;
and patch 1160's rule also holds for a line and a conic whose edges leave
their common vertex in the same direction (mitcad#2050).

The case (GitHub #466): a plate with rounded corners and a cylinder joined
on its top on the axis of one corner, of the corner's radius, so that the
cylinder continues a quarter of the corner's side (one face after the join,
or two faces on one surface). The concave edge round the cylinder's foot
then runs three quarters round, from one end of that quarter to the other,
and the plate's top ends in a point of no angle at each of its ends: the
top's straight edge along the plate's side and the concave arc leave the
point in the same direction, and below it the side meets the cylinder
tangentially. The shell of the plate with the cylinder's top and the
plate's bottom removed, inside or outside, and its closed offsets, were not
done (the thick solid with fewer faces than the body: BRepOffset_UnknownError,
"the offset faces do not close up") with the intersection join, or not
valid with the foot's faces apart; with the arc join they were valid, and so
were the offsets of the same plate with a smaller cylinder on the axis, or
with a larger corner (the foot's edge a whole circle).

BRepOffset_Inter3d intersects the offsets of the top and the cylinder,
enlarged, into a whole circle (its origin where the enlarged cylinder's
seam cuts it), and TrimEdge cut it to the range from the smallest to the
largest parameter of the vertices on it: from the origin to one end of the
foot's edge, over the corner quarter the foot does not run along, instead
of the three quarters across the origin. TrimEdge now takes the edges the
intersection is the image of (the new parameter theBuild, the map of
intersections of the edges); for an edge all round a periodic curve, the new
TrimOverOrigins splits the curve at its vertices into arcs, keeps those whose
middles project inside the range of one of those edges (IsOverOrigins), and
trims the edge to them where they make one run round the curve and not all
of it. Where the run crosses the origin and a vertex lies there (where the
cylinder's seam cuts the circle), the edge is cut there in two, the edge up
to that vertex and a second from it (the new parameter theSecond; TrimEdges
adds the second to the intersections of the edges the first is the image
of, to the new edges and to their origins): one edge across the origin lay
past the period of the cylinder's offset on it, its face invalid. TrimEdges
and TrimEdge no longer remove from AsDes, as a piece without intersections,
an edge whose vertices are in AsDes (BRepAlgo_AsDes::Remove throws
Standard_ConstructionError on it, which with the split ended every offset
of the case). Where the
arcs are no such run, where the run is the range from the smallest to the
largest parameter, where it crosses the origin with no vertex there, and
for edges that do not run all round a periodic curve, it is as before.

At the points of no angle, BRepOffset_Inter2d::ConnexIntByInt leaves the
images of the top's two edges on the top's offset, a line and a circle
touching there, unintersected with each other, as 1160 does for curves that
are not lines or conics (the new LeaveInOneDirection: the edges' tangents,
pointing away from their common vertex, within 1e-3 rad of each other); the
vertex is where each crosses the offset of the tangential edge below. Where
the edges leave the vertex in opposite directions (a rounding between planes
cut by a plane, the images continuing each other) it is as before.
MeetAlongTangentEdge also takes such a vertex with a fourth edge, a seam
of one of the faces beyond the two running on from the tangential edge:
in the reporter's design the merged face's seam starts at one of the
points of no angle (its corner's arc starts there), and the line and the
circle were intersected there, tangentially. There the walk round the
cylinder's offset also passes from the seam to the tangential edge it runs
on from; neither has an image of its own, and ConnexIntByInt put their
common vertex, the offset of the original one (10 mm up), on both offset
edges, which lie on one line: the tangential edge's offset then reached
past where the offset of the foot crosses that line (8 mm up), into the
seam's, and the shell was not done (the closed offset 442 mm3 instead of
24035). The new SeamRunsOnFromTangentEdge leaves that vertex out where
another edge of the face at the vertex has an image: the offset edges get
their ends where the foot's offset and the top's cross them, from the walks
round the cylinder's offset and round the side's.

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2050.
